    {
      "question": "e-8157",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "5a71a1bde1426a9c222b57d1adab1c894ce9f29ec6efcf8253bfeced2e77328a",
      "new_sha256": "d23a9e735e9ff2dd545bdeb6210022d86d262ac691a78a277e9bc2e530ca0683",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-8157",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PDP^T$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the first column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties.",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/example-8157.json",
          "file_sha256": "0bb3e2b0ddb96ae94c4e4fb03fecde025763317c1db25c053f79ee475ec3ac07"
        }
      ],
