          "value": "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD=P^TAP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=5\\quad$ and $\\quad \\lambda_2=-8$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For $\\lambda_1=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/7b0367066950407149521e6b06255f71b25fe5e67bc609fac5e914d3d1315fe7.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{13},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/498ecfed1cfe5ed4b1df8c02088045e67c4d5601e47f403288d271447c5ded28.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]+8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/0aa3aecabd941ccc8e176aef479eb3630197ff9c7fb6ca63e028e41315c9aafb.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{13},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/1f494272dcd6828d437e2f566e46aa5b5ece49a92cd82af094e25834fe1bbcbe.png)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14075254/assets/737d19f3df39ef56518c98843cb7dd0359965570892b17df57fb4117bb0f5844.png)",
